    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": "Lambda Function"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "A Lambda function is just like a regular function with a couple of differences:"
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet_list",
      "items": [
        "A lambda function doesn't have a name",
        "The body of a lambda function can have only one expression"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "To better understand lambda functions, let's look at a regular function and then rewrite it using a lambda function."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "content": "1def double(n):\n2    return n*2\n3\n4print(double(10))    # 20"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Here, the double() function takes a single argument and returns its double."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Now, let's write the same function using lambda."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "content": "1double = lambda n: n*2\n2print(double(10))    # 20"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "In the code above:"
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet_list",
      "items": [
        "We use the lambda keyword to define lambda functions",
        "The variable before the : is the argument (\"n\").",
        "The variable after the : is the return value(\"n*2\")."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Syntax of Lambda function",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "In the program, the lambda function is assigned to the double variable. Now, to call this lambda function, we have used this code: double(value)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "In the next exercise, you'll solve a problem where you'll get to apply lambda functions to create an efficient solution."
    },
